1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:06,325
We now extend the workflow beyond the browser. The terminal exercises use IBM's real

2
00:00:06,337 --> 00:00:12,688
Python TNZ library to communicate over a socket with a small local TN3270 simulator.

3
00:00:13,550 --> 00:00:17,938
The simulator displays a synthetic transfer lookup screen and queries the same account

4
00:00:17,950 --> 00:00:23,412
data as our web application. This is practical cross-protocol automation, not a

5
00:00:23,425 --> 00:00:28,513
connection to a real mainframe. You will understand the boundary, install the prepared

6
00:00:28,525 --> 00:00:33,050
client environment, and verify terminal readiness through an actual screen exchange.

7
00:00:35,262 --> 00:00:41,337
Playwright controls the browser and its HTTP observations. TNZ controls the terminal

8
00:00:41,350 --> 00:00:47,512
session. The hybrid test coordinates both tools through a transfer reference. Trying

9
00:00:47,524 --> 00:00:51,200
to locate a terminal field with a browser locator would confuse two different

10
00:00:51,212 --> 00:00:55,824
interfaces, unless your actual application provided a browser-based terminal with its

11
00:00:55,837 --> 00:01:01,937
own separate contract. Our laboratory uses a protocol client directly. Keep the

12
00:01:01,950 --> 00:01:06,374
boundary visible in the architecture diagram so each failure points to the tool and

13
00:01:06,387 --> 00:01:09,700
connection that actually produced its evidence during the test run.

14
00:01:11,912 --> 00:01:17,112
The terminal screen reads a local lookup endpoint using the transfer reference. That

15
00:01:17,124 --> 00:01:21,287
endpoint returns the authoritative in-memory transfer and current account balance from

16
00:01:21,300 --> 00:01:26,624
the accounts service. It does not query an independent mainframe database, and it does

17
00:01:26,637 --> 00:01:31,687
not prove durable persistence. The distributed ledger projection remains a separate

18
00:01:31,699 --> 00:01:36,462
observation from the earlier episode. This distinction matters when describing

19
00:01:36,474 --> 00:01:41,649
coverage: the terminal exercise checks reference transport, field interaction, screen

20
00:01:41,662 --> 00:01:46,424
parsing, and agreement across interfaces that expose the same synthetic data source.

21
00:01:48,658 --> 00:01:53,920
Open the mainframe laboratory directory beside the distributed project. The simulator

22
00:01:53,933 --> 00:01:59,646
file owns the server-side terminal behavior. The adapter file wraps the real TNZ

23
00:01:59,658 --> 00:02:04,583
client with a session lifecycle, bounded waits, input validation, and structured

24
00:02:04,596 --> 00:02:10,333
output. Terminal protocol tests exercise this boundary with deterministic data, while

25
00:02:10,346 --> 00:02:15,508
the hybrid browser test uses the running distributed services. Read the requirements

26
00:02:15,520 --> 00:02:20,583
files before installation. Their separation lets you install a small terminal-only

27
00:02:20,596 --> 00:02:24,558
environment or include the browser dependencies required for the complete hybrid

28
00:02:24,570 --> 00:02:25,458
exercise.

29
00:02:27,684 --> 00:02:32,634
Run the supplied setup script from the mainframe directory. Use the browser option

30
00:02:32,646 --> 00:02:37,372
when you plan to execute the Python hybrid case, because terminal-only setup does not

31
00:02:37,384 --> 00:02:43,184
provide every browser dependency. Installation downloads packages and, when requested,

32
00:02:43,296 --> 00:02:48,972
Chromium; the completed exercise communicates only with local services. Finish those

33
00:02:48,984 --> 00:02:53,896
downloads before the live workshop. If your approved environment already supplies

34
00:02:53,909 --> 00:02:57,922
Edge, use the documented browser-channel option in the test runner rather than

35
00:02:57,934 --> 00:03:02,347
assuming a Chromium executable exists just because a browser opens normally on your

36
00:03:02,359 --> 00:03:03,046
desktop.

37
00:03:05,270 --> 00:03:09,682
First start the distributed application using its documented synthetic hook value.

38
00:03:10,545 --> 00:03:14,695
Then start the terminal simulator in a separate terminal from the mainframe directory.

39
00:03:15,558 --> 00:03:20,570
Its default address is loopback on port twenty-three twenty-three. Leave that terminal

40
00:03:20,582 --> 00:03:25,658
running while the adapter connects. If the port is already occupied, inspect the

41
00:03:25,670 --> 00:03:30,945
conflict instead of stopping an unrelated application. The laboratory startup and

42
00:03:30,957 --> 00:03:36,295
shutdown instructions target only its own processes. Keeping these terminals separate

43
00:03:36,308 --> 00:03:40,308
also makes it easier to identify which service reported a connection or lookup

44
00:03:40,320 --> 00:03:40,908
failure.

45
00:03:43,120 --> 00:03:49,070
Run the adapter's health action with a bounded timeout. This check opens a TNZ session

46
00:03:49,082 --> 00:03:53,895
and waits for the expected screen, rather than treating an open TCP port as sufficient

47
00:03:53,908 --> 00:03:59,258
readiness. A ready terminal does not prove the gateway is available: the simulator can

48
00:03:59,270 --> 00:04:04,395
display its initial panel while business lookup remains unavailable. Record the health

49
00:04:04,408 --> 00:04:09,382
result and then verify the distributed services separately. When you later submit a

50
00:04:09,395 --> 00:04:13,457
reference, any backend-unavailable status belongs to a different stage from

51
00:04:13,470 --> 00:04:15,732
establishing the terminal connection itself.

52
00:04:17,946 --> 00:04:23,258
Inspect the initial screen snapshot and find the transfer reference input. This local

53
00:04:23,271 --> 00:04:27,458
panel deliberately has one reference field and labeled result lines for status,

54
00:04:27,608 --> 00:04:32,784
amount, currency, source account, and balance. Those labels are part of the

55
00:04:32,796 --> 00:04:38,796
simulator's contract. They are not universal mainframe field names. In the next

56
00:04:38,808 --> 00:04:43,408
episode, we will inspect how the adapter positions the cursor, clears the input,

57
00:04:43,646 --> 00:04:48,271
enters the reference, and waits for a result associated with that exact value before

58
00:04:48,284 --> 00:04:50,708
parsing any business fields from the screen.

59
00:04:52,940 --> 00:04:57,665
Before creating a transfer, run a lookup using a valid reference that does not exist

60
00:04:57,678 --> 00:05:03,640
in the current dataset. The expected business result is not found. This is different

61
00:05:03,653 --> 00:05:07,590
from failing to connect or reaching a deadline without receiving a usable panel.

62
00:05:08,452 --> 00:05:14,628
Inspect the adapter's JSON output and exit status together. Its command-line contract

63
00:05:14,640 --> 00:05:19,340
distinguishes successful readiness or completion, business results such as not found,

64
00:05:19,565 --> 00:05:25,015
and operational failures. Preserving these categories makes a hybrid test report much

65
00:05:25,028 --> 00:05:27,528
more useful than a single generic terminal error.

66
00:05:29,758 --> 00:05:34,108
The classroom adapter uses an unencrypted connection only to loopback and rejects

67
00:05:34,120 --> 00:05:39,645
nonlocal destinations. That is part of this simulator's design, not a template for

68
00:05:39,658 --> 00:05:44,633
connecting to an external host. An authorized real environment would need its own

69
00:05:44,645 --> 00:05:50,908
host, screen contract, test records, and verified transport configuration. None of

70
00:05:50,920 --> 00:05:55,933
those are established by this lesson. Keeping the local boundary explicit allows you

71
00:05:55,946 --> 00:06:00,070
to learn field automation and orchestration without suggesting that a successful

72
00:06:00,083 --> 00:06:05,096
synthetic lookup validates access, security, or behavior on an actual mainframe

73
00:06:05,108 --> 00:06:05,833
platform.

74
00:06:08,064 --> 00:06:13,826
Execute the standalone terminal tests before the full hybrid path. These tests launch

75
00:06:13,839 --> 00:06:17,714
their own local server with deterministic fixture data and communicate through the

76
00:06:17,726 --> 00:06:24,189
actual TNZ library over a real socket. They help isolate connection, field, and status

77
00:06:24,201 --> 00:06:29,989
handling from browser or gateway issues. Inspect the actual test count and result file

78
00:06:30,001 --> 00:06:35,464
instead of assuming the suite passed because it exists. A successful protocol suite

79
00:06:35,476 --> 00:06:39,976
supports the adapter boundary; the browser-to-terminal workflow still requires its own

80
00:06:39,989 --> 00:06:42,089
run against the distributed application.

81
00:06:44,306 --> 00:06:49,068
Explain the complete lookup path to a partner: TNZ sends a reference to the local

82
00:06:49,081 --> 00:06:53,544
simulator, which calls the gateway lookup endpoint and displays shared account data.

83
00:06:54,406 --> 00:06:59,518
Then show your actual readiness result and protocol test outcome. Identify which

84
00:06:59,531 --> 00:07:04,644
components were running and which were unnecessary for the standalone tests. If an

85
00:07:04,656 --> 00:07:09,894
installation or connection failed, keep that checkpoint unresolved. You are prepared

86
00:07:09,906 --> 00:07:14,231
for the next episode when you can distinguish terminal protocol behavior, business

87
00:07:14,244 --> 00:07:18,244
lookup behavior, and the limits of this deliberately simulated environment.
